title: Strict serializability unifies transaction and operation consistency hierarchies
source: hn
url: https://jepsen.io/consistency/models
date: '2026-11-20'
authors:
- alihm
why_read: Read this to understand the formal hierarchy of distributed consistency models and how transactional guarantees relate to single-object operations.
hn_id: '50571287'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50571287
image: /infographics/04-hn-50383259.jpg
---

Understanding distributed consistency models is often hampered by overloaded terminology and subtle distinctions between single-object and multi-object guarantees. The Jepsen consistency reference formalizes these boundaries into an explicit hierarchy, charting exactly what anomalies each model permits.

At the peak stands strict serializability, which combines transactional serializability across multi-object operations with external consistency across real time. Dropping real-time ordering yields serializability, which decomposes further through snapshot isolation and cursor stability down to read committed. On the single-object axis, linearizability cascades into sequential and causal consistency, each trading away ordering constraints to maintain availability during network partitions.

This breakdown clarifies exactly which guarantees require global coordination versus those that can remain available under asynchronous networks. It serves as an essential architectural compass whenever you are evaluating database guarantees or designing state replication protocols.

