title: Merkle tree certificates reduce overhead for post-quantum signatures
source: hn
url: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-plants-merkle-tree-certs/
date: '2026-11-20'
authors:
- David Benjamin
- Devon O'Brien
- Bas Westerbaan
- Luke Valenta
- Filippo Valsorda
why_read: Read this specification to understand how integrating public logging directly into certificates reduces transmission overhead. It provides a viable mechanism to accommodate shorter certificate lifespans and bulky post-quantum signature schemes.
hn_id: '50583134'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50583134
image: /infographics/04-hn-50583134.jpg
---

Merkle Tree Certificates propose to replace traditional public-key infrastructure signatures on the web with compact cryptographic inclusion proofs.

Under the current X.509 model, every TLS certificate carries an explicit digital signature from an issuing Certificate Authority, followed by separate Certificate Transparency SCT timestamps. As the industry moves toward short-lived certificates and significantly larger post-quantum cryptographic keys, transmitting these heavy signatures over the wire on every TLS handshake introduces massive bandwidth and latency overheads.

The new IETF specification from engineers at Google, Apple, and Cloudflare flips this paradigm. Instead of signing individual certificates, authorities batch thousands of certificates into an append-only Merkle tree and sign only the tree head. Clients verify certificate validity by checking a compact Merkle inclusion proof against a known log state.

This architectural shift allows relying parties to verify short-lived certificates without sending individual signatures over the wire. It simultaneously enforces Certificate Transparency by design rather than as an external bolt-on.

Engineering around post-quantum key bloat requires redesigning protocols at the transport boundary before latency budgets collapse.

