date: '2026-11-20'
authors:
- marvin-hansen
why_read: Read this to understand how eliminating heap allocations and async runtimes using io_uring unlocks full hardware throughput. You will learn the mechanics of a high-performance thread-per-core storage engine in Rust.
hn_id: '50571449'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50571449
image: /infographics/05-hn-50571449.jpg
---

Standard cloud storage SDKs frequently leave tens of gigabits of NIC and NVMe bandwidth stranded. Blocking DNS queries, hidden background thread pools, and relentless heap allocations create latency spikes and memory fragmentation that bottleneck high-throughput storage engines.

Longshore takes an alternative approach by pairing Rust directly with a strict thread-per-core io_uring architecture. There is no asynchronous runtime overhead and no locks along the hot data path. Each CPU core controls its own dedicated completion queue, handling all network sockets, NVMe file slots, and timeouts independently.

Memory management follows a zero-allocation discipline: buffers and tables allocate once during startup, after which an allocator guard seals the heap and panics on any allocation attempt. Inter-core communication avoids shared mutexes entirely, relying instead on bounded, fixed-size mailboxes.

The benchmark results prove the value of this hardware-conscious design. A single Longshore thread running on an AWS Graviton4 instance with a 300 Gbit card achieved an upload rate of 36.4 GB/s directly to Amazon S3, or 16.7 GB/s while running on-the-fly CRC-64/NVME checksum calculations.

Eliminating hidden runtime abstractions and designing strictly around hardware mechanical sympathy remains the most reliable path to extreme systems throughput.

