title: How hole punching in sparse files optimizes local S3 caching
source: hn
url: https://www.bzrk.dev/blog/sparse-files/
date: '2026-11-20'
authors:
- Kresten Krab Thorup
why_read: Read this to understand how Linux sparse files and hole punching allow systems to reclaim disk space efficiently when caching remote objects. You will learn the low-level mechanics of storing partial files without wasting storage on unneeded data.
hn_id: '50572507'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50572507
image: /infographics/06-hn-50572507.jpg
---

Caching object storage data on local SSDs usually forces a painful trade-off: either cache full remote files and exhaust disk space, or manage complex block-level chunking metadata in application memory.

The team building Berserk sidestepped this trade-off by turning to a classic Unix capability: sparse files and kernel-level hole punching via fallocate with FALLOC_FL_PUNCH_HOLE.

When a query worker reads a specific byte range from an S3 object, the engine lazily writes that range into a local sparse file at the exact matching offset. The logical file size appears identical to the remote object, but unread regions consume zero physical disk blocks. As cached ranges fall out of active use, the system punches holes back into the file, allowing the filesystem to immediately reclaim storage blocks without deleting or resizing the file descriptor.

Because the byte offsets match the remote object directly, query workers avoid translating offsets through an extra chunk-mapping layer. Instead, worker processes can read directly from the cached file via standard pread calls across process boundaries.

Lean directly on native kernel filesystem primitives rather than reimplementing complex block allocations in user space.

