For over a decade, running Redis under heavy write loads meant preparing for memory spikes during snapshots. Because snapshotting relied strictly on Linux fork and copy-on-write mechanisms, a busy database could easily double its memory footprint and trigger out-of-memory crashes.

Valkey 9.2 introduces forkless snapshots and full synchronization by executing the snapshot on a dedicated background thread. This architecture costs a negligible four additional bytes per key at startup, but it completely eliminates the copy-on-write memory overhead that plagues traditional fork-based architectures.

In addition to forkless persistence, this release incorporates streaming LZ4 replication compression and cuts sorted set memory overhead by thirty-six to forty-three percent. Valkey also prioritizes cluster heartbeats and throttles writes before replica lag causes an expensive resynchronization.

Moving snapshot isolation from the operating system page table to the storage engine is a massive architectural improvement for in-memory systems.

