Most AI agent memory systems make one of two mistakes. They either log verbose step-by-step action trajectories that clutter the context window, or they only record successful paths and discard failed executions.

ReasoningBank addresses this by extracting abstract, transferable heuristics from both successful attempts and mistakes. When an agent fails on an environment constraint, the system extracts the underlying tactical failure rather than the raw tool call. It stores these lessons into an evolving memory bank that guides future reasoning.

During evaluations across complex browsing and software engineering benchmarks, this dual-feedback mechanism demonstrated sustained self-improvement without requiring weight fine-tuning. The agent avoids repeating its previous mistakes because it retrieves strategic guidance before executing downstream actions.

True agent durability depends on learning from failure instead of pretending it never happened.

