Most LLM inference serving optimizations still assume grouped-query attention as the default baseline. However, modern open weights models have quietly shifted toward hybrid architectures combining latent caches, sliding window mechanisms, compressed representations, and linear attention.

Benchmarking eleven recent open models against a comparable grouped-query attention baseline at 128K tokens reveals a massive performance divergence. DeepSeek V4.1 Flash retains up to 139 times less attention state in memory, moves 250 times fewer bytes per decode step, and accelerates decoding attention by 44 times. On prefill workloads, architectures like Qwen3.8 Flash Next reduce attention FLOP requirements by a factor of 183.

These architectural shifts fundamentally alter inference profiling. When KV cache memory footprints collapse by two orders of magnitude, memory bandwidth constraints relax, shifting bottlenecks from memory capacity back to compute and communication. Serving infrastructure built strictly around grouped-query attention assumptions will soon optimize for the wrong constraints.

The bottleneck in high-throughput inference is shifting faster than the serving frameworks designed to host it.
